<P>
<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
The concept behind Robot Arena is a great one.  There is a lot of potential to have an awesome game that is very realistic.  The problem lies in the fact that the game was a low budget game and thus, the overall quality of the game decreased and the expectations of the game were not met.  I would love to see what they could have done with a larger budget.
<P>
The game was fairly easy to understand. There were some down points such as assigning hard to remember keyboard commands for various items placed on the robot.  I found that I always had to adjust the commands for my robot before I would take it into the arena for the first time.  The game also only supported keyboard controls while in the arena and thus didn’t offer a game pad as an option.
<P>
As far as the robots and their functionality go, the game was ok.  There are quite a few different accessories that are made available to the user. You can choose from quite a few different weapons, various chassis, some armor, accessories, and the various types of wheels.  Each robot could be different, and in order to build more robots, you would need to win money.  (This wasn’t hard as you could start a tournament with $1,000 and end with $40,000+)  It would be nice to see additional add-ons to the game that would allow you to choose from more accessories for your robot.
<P>
The graphics were ok, although with more money invested in the game, they could have been a whole lot better.  Many of the effects could defiantly be improved rather than just having a part blow up. By making a change to the configuration file, you can get your opponent to actually bump when you hit them but that is about as far as the effects go.  You will see a little damage to the chassis but for the most part the items will just blow up.
<P>
Robot Arena built in a couple of different game types, which help with the longetivity of the game.  The practice section is where you would go when you are first learning and trying out the game.  The tournament mode is where you would go to start with the “easy” robots and move up to the “hard” robots.  The problem was, I was able to create a robot with some of the basic gear, enter the tournament, and defeat all the robots in one big swoop.  The robots didn’t really seem to get that much harder and thus the game seemed way to easy.  Hopefully their upcoming patch to improve the AI for single player mode and make the game slightly more difficult.
<P>
Due to the lack of difficulty on the pre-designed robots, I looked forward to playing online against some other gamers.  The problem is, I could never get it to work. You have to know someone else whom you want to play against.  I didn’t know anyone else who has the game and thus, I was not able to play against him or her.  I was hoping to be able to see a list of people who are hosting a game and then choose who I want to compete against.  The Online section of the game is what would of made this game the best as you wouldn’t be limited to the computer robots but instead would be able to play against other gamers.  Unfortunately, I was never able to get this to work.
